{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786510219,"nonce":"SeiR1xo1hgsaojauNTbnDT0Lug0=","target":"2f9d7f04827d9a5837ca3a3afb300d8993a838531fa5b497f5b4a3494794f5e7","message":"Title: `GET /alert-groups/{id}` still reads every member row and sorts it in Go, which two comments describe as already fixed\n\n**What is wrong.** Two comments describe the full-membership-fetch-and-slice-in-Go pattern in the past tense, as a thing that was replaced. It is still what the detail endpoint does.\n\n`internal/grouping/repository/member.go:288-295`, on the keyset query built to replace it: *\"The handler used to read EVERY member through `Get()` and slice the result in Go: correct for a group of forty, a full membership fetch for a storm of five thousand, and a page whose `has_more` was computed from a list the caller had already paid to materialise.\"*\n\n`internal/grouping/service/read.go:138-143`, on `Service.Members`: *\"the handler could not call it at all and reached for `Get().Members` instead, materialising the whole membership and slicing it in Go.\"*\n\nBoth are accurate about `GET /alert-groups/{id}/alerts`, which now uses `Service.Members` → `ListCurrentMembers` (`internal/grouping/api/handlers.go:338`, `service/read.go:147`, `repository/member.go:296-302`). Neither is accurate about `GET /alert-groups/{id}`. `Service.Get` still calls `CurrentMembers` (`service/read.go:113`), which is `currentMembersSQL` — `WHERE org_id = $1 AND group_id = $2 AND left_at IS NULL ORDER BY joined_at ASC`, no `LIMIT` (`repository/member.go:138-142`). `detailDTO` then sorts the entire returned slice and breaks at twenty (`api/handlers.go:424-427`), and `sortedMembers` copies the whole slice before sorting it (`:501-509`).\n\n**The demonstration.** The scale figure is the code's own. `member.go:293-294` names \"a storm of five thousand\" as the case the fetch is wrong for, and `maxTopAlerts = 20` (`api/handlers.go:20`) is how many of those five thousand rows reach the response. At the storm the code itself describes, one detail render transfers 5 000 member rows, allocates a 5 000-element copy, sorts it, and discards 4 980 of them.\n\nIt is not one render. Four handlers call `rt.svc.Get`: the detail read at `api/handlers.go:66`, and the ack, snooze and unsnooze responses at `:110`, `:199` and `:240`. Every group action therefore pays the full membership fetch again to render its reply — and a storm is exactly when a human is pressing those buttons.\n\n`AllMembers` (`repository/member.go:159-163`) has the same shape with the `left_at IS NULL` filter removed, so it reads every membership row the generation has ever had, joined or departed. It backs `History` (`service/read.go:159`) and `MembersAt` (`:169`), and `MembersAt` then filters the whole set in Go with `WasMemberAt` (`:173-178`) — an instant the SQL could have applied.\n\n`ListCurrentMembers`'s own planner note assumes the bound this path lacks: *\"A generation holds at most a few thousand members, so this is a bounded index range\"* (`member.go:306-309`). Nothing enforces that ceiling, and `currentMembersSQL` does not even ask for one.\n\n**Why it matters.** The bounded keyset query, the clamp and the paginated route all already exist; `detailDTO`'s own doc comment claims it renders \"a bounded member preview\" (`api/handlers.go:408-409`). The preview is bounded. The read behind it is not, and the two comments quoted above make it easy to believe this was already dealt with.\n\n**Where.** `internal/grouping/service/read.go:108-127` (`Get`), `:166-180` (`MembersAt`), `internal/grouping/repository/member.go:138-142` (`currentMembersSQL`) and `:159-163` (`allMembersSQL`), `internal/grouping/api/handlers.go:66,110,199,240` (the four callers), `:424-427` and `:501-509` (the in-Go sort), `:20` (`maxTopAlerts`).\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** `Service.Get` obtains its preview through the existing `ListCurrentMembers` with `limit = maxTopAlerts`, so `detailDTO` no longer sorts a slice it did not need, and `MembersAt` pushes the instant into SQL instead of filtering `AllMembers` in Go. The two past-tense comments then describe reality.","files":null}]}